---------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Distributed Sequence/ID Generation Service — Banking Platform
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Problem
  A banking platform needed globally unique, roughly-ordered IDs across
  dozens of services. The legacy approach hit the database on every
  request — a throughput ceiling and a single point of failure on the
  hottest path in the system.

Design (segment-based allocation, designed by me)
  - Segment allocation: each node claims a numbered range (segment)
    from the database in one transaction, then serves IDs from memory,
    cutting DB round-trips by ~99%.
  - Dynamic segment sizing: segment length auto-scales with consumption
    rate — hot keys get larger segments (fewer DB hits), idle keys
    shrink back (less ID waste on restart).
  - Double-buffering: the next segment is fetched asynchronously before
    the current one is exhausted, so allocation never blocks on the DB;
    a DB outage is survivable for as long as the buffer lasts, giving
    operations a recovery window.
  - Failure semantics: a node crash wastes at most one segment —
    uniqueness is never violated.

Result
  Tens of thousands of IDs/sec per node, sequence-table DB load reduced
  by ~99%, zero duplicate-ID incidents in production.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Navis Go — AI-Powered Desktop Development Tool (Solo Build)
---------------------------------------------------------------------

A cross-platform desktop shell for AI-assisted coding: streaming agent
conversation, tool-call inspection, integrated terminal, workspace and
file management, multi-provider LLM routing, and an Extension system.

  Stack:  Tauri 2 (Rust, tokio) + SolidJS + TypeScript (strict) +
          CodeMirror 6 + xterm.js
  Scale:  ~500 source files / 150K+ lines, with product docs, design
          system, automated verification scripts, and CI guard tests

Architecture: a true microkernel
  The Rust backend is built on a kernel of six generic primitives —
  Registry (capability discovery/lifecycle), Pipeline (staged execution
  with retry/backoff/cancellation), EventBus (topic+scope routing with
  backpressure), Policy Engine (Allow/Ask/Deny authorization),
  Audit (tamper-evident records with digest/redaction), and
  Observability (trace/span execution events).

  All business domains (Agent state machine, LLM gateway, MCP tool
  engine, Extension system, session management) are capabilities composed
  on top of the kernel. A CI-enforced boundary test statically verifies
  the kernel never references business vocabulary — the dependency
  arrow only points one way, permanently.

How it differs from Claude Code / OpenCode / Codex-style tools
  Those are excellent agent products; Navis Go explores a different
  question — what if the agent runtime were an operating system rather
  than an application?

  - Tool execution as kernel pipeline, not hardcoded loop: every tool
    call runs through the same staged pipeline with uniform retry,
    cancellation, and observability — adding a tool never means
    touching the execution engine.
  - Authorization as a first-class kernel object: permission decisions
    (Allow/Ask/Deny) come from a prioritized constraint engine, not
    scattered if-statements. Policies are inspectable, composable, and
    auditable — a design habit carried over from banking systems.
  - Audit by construction: every significant action produces a
    tamper-evident audit record with size-bounded digests and
    redaction support. You can always answer "what exactly did the
    agent do, and who approved it?"
  - Extensibility without forking: Extensions contribute providers,
    middlewares, MCP servers, tools, UI views, themes, and keybindings
    through 16 registry-backed contribution types — the same mechanism
    the built-in features use.

Core value
  Trustworthy autonomy: the user can see what the agent is doing,
  gate risky actions through explicit policy, and audit everything
  after the fact — without giving up execution speed.

